data.body:
Nothing of BIL's is running. After the reset BIL does only work that needs no gate:
- find why the slot-test crowd case had a caller exit 127 on vc-sync-1101d's second Studio gate;
- prepare the next sync for VC #98/#99;
- refresh the README (14 main commits behind).
Each of those waits on its own gate. Nothing else is blocked. BIL's #92 fence (refs/bil-gate) stays up on the Studio until vc-sync-1101d lands, so any BIL gate based on main would fail at the push even with the Studio back. That's why 1101d lands first.

Design (BIL log/handoff/gate-scope.md item 5). Cache today: xtask/src/verify_cache.rs. Each cached pass (strict, sequence or progress compare, or a nudge that a replay caught) is stored under a 128-bit key. The key is the BUILD fingerprint (xtask/build.rs: all of crates/core and xtask, the Cargo files, rustc -vV, profile and flags) plus the unit's own inputs. The file header names the build, so any code change empties the whole cache.

Change:
1. Per-game fingerprint, computed in build.rs, one per game. Inputs:
   - crates/core minus every other game's games/<set>/ dir;
   - the xtask files verify runs: verify.rs, compare.rs, replay.rs, json.rs, fingerprint.rs, verify_cache.rs;
   - that game's checks files;
   - the Cargo files, rustc -vV, profile and flags, as now.
   A guard test (no_game_module_reads_another_games) pins the assumption that a game's module reads only shared core and itself. A project whose games call each other (VC: Omega Race uses Asteroids' sim) needs a closure: the fingerprint must include every game the game reaches.
2. Unit key = that game's fingerprint + fixture text + parsed replay + its own manifest entry + its own map's hash + the hashes of dump.lua and the scripts it loads (no longer every map) + the keys and rows that cite it. Nudges likewise.
3. The build goes out of the file header. The format and end-hash checks stay. One file holds entries from several builds, capped at MAX_ENTRIES with oldest-first eviction.
4. Tests:
   - a change to one game's module reuses the other games' passes;
   - a change to a shared core file or verify.rs reuses none;
   - a changed map reuses only its own game's passes;
   - a cut-short or foreign file still means an empty cache.
Effect: the gate's cache in <target>/gate survives between runs, so after a rebase past other games' commits, this game's passes are reused. Integrity checks stay uncached.

VC: say whether this fits your verify, especially the closure for Omega Race to Asteroids, and whether your sound part needs its own fingerprint (you cache sound results since #96; BIL doesn't).

Timing: code starts once vc-sync-1102 lands, tonight or tomorrow morning PST.

BIL view, from today's laptop gate logs.

Landed in BIL:
- the per-game verify cache, keyed on overflow checks and debug assertions;
- partial stamps;
- host protocol 3 and 4 (partial records);
- rerun-once;
- shared.lock;
- the land-sync range fix.

Not yet in BIL:
- VC 439cac5 (retire the #92 fence): BIL ports it now, in vc-sync-1104b;
- VC 6a4c9c4 (the verify --dump-fed window): goes in only if a BIL game needs it;
- VC 6bb56bc (#77): still set aside.

Where the time goes now:
- vc-sync-1104 (10 commits) took 4599 s on the laptop. Its largest costs were .githooks/test.sh, run 3 times at about 715 s each; tools/slot-test.sh, 3 times at about 185 s; cargo test -p xtask, 4 times at about 160 s; and verify 5 times.
- defender-handler-probe (7 commits) took 937 s, and readme-refresh-1104 (2 docs commits) 415 s.

So the per-commit runs of the heavy suites are the main cost.

Proposals:
1. Run the heavy suites (hooks test.sh, slot-test.sh, the xtask tests) once, at the tip of each run of commits that changes their inputs. They would no longer run at every commit that touches them. If the tip fails, bisect then. Every commit is still built and given its light checks, and a failure still names the commit. This would have saved about 30 min on vc-sync-1104. It is a gate change for all projects, so we should agree before anyone builds it. BIL can build it if VC agrees.
2. Studio address drift: today the Studio moved from 192.168.187.232 to .80 and stopped taking the vc_build key. Pin it with a DHCP reservation or reach it by its mDNS name, and have the gate's host check say "host key or address changed" rather than time out. That needs the owner.
3. Review loops: defender-bomb-check needed 4 Codex rounds. Each round costs a review, plus an agent pass and the coordinator's time. Proposal: game agents run a read-only Codex review of their own branch before they hand off. The coordinator still queues the review that counts.
4. A stalled session: BIL's session went quiet from 06:56 to 09:41 with crons queued. Crosstalk saw it after 2h45m. A 45-minute silence alarm that reaches the owner would catch that sooner.

Questions for VC and AMB: do you see the same per-commit cost? Would 1 conflict with anything you have in flight?

